1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,100
A hundred monkeys at the stock exchange. Every day, each monkey flips

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,535
a coin: buy the S and P 500, or sell it.

3
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:09,008
Five hundred trading days later, on real S

4
00:00:09,108 --> 00:00:11,130
and P data, here are their fortunes.

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,111
One of them has earned a t-statistic of two point eight. Taken alone, that

6
00:00:16,211 --> 00:00:20,242
is a p-value of three in a thousand. Is this monkey a genius?

7
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:24,508
The question is not whether the best monkey looks good. It is

8
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:28,051
whether the best of a hundred monkeys would look this good anyway.

9
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:34,987
Turn each monkey into its t-statistic. They pile up into

10
00:00:35,087 --> 00:00:38,871
the bell curve of no skill, exactly as chance predicts.

11
00:00:38,971 --> 00:00:42,402
A handful clear one point six four five, the usual

12
00:00:42,502 --> 00:00:45,792
five percent bar. The best one clears it easily.

13
00:00:46,866 --> 00:00:51,347
Here is why. One monkey's t-statistic is standard normal: it clears

14
00:00:51,447 --> 00:00:54,970
one point six four five five percent of the time. The

15
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:58,731
best of m monkeys has its own distribution, in red, and

16
00:00:58,831 --> 00:01:01,055
as m grows it slides to the right.

17
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:04,985
With a hundred monkeys, the best one clears the usual bar ninety-nine

18
00:01:05,085 --> 00:01:08,421
times in a hundred. The honest five percent bar for the best

19
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:11,743
of a hundred independent monkeys is three point two eight.

20
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:16,956
Real rules are correlated, and returns echo, so in practice we

21
00:01:17,056 --> 00:01:21,452
measure the bar by bootstrap. Cut history into blocks, keeping the

22
00:01:21,552 --> 00:01:25,402
echoes inside each, and reshuffle them into a new history.

23
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,296
Re-centre every monkey so that none has skill, and

24
00:01:29,396 --> 00:01:33,187
record the best t-statistic. Repeat four hundred times, and

25
00:01:33,287 --> 00:01:35,560
the crowd of best monkeys builds up.

26
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:38,878
Our genius lands in the middle of that crowd. About a

27
00:01:38,978 --> 00:01:42,665
quarter of the worlds without skill produce a best monkey at

28
00:01:42,765 --> 00:01:46,516
least this good. That share is the p-value of White's Reality

29
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:50,240
Check, and of Hansen's test of superior predictive ability.

30
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:55,470
A different question: not whether anyone beats a benchmark, but which

31
00:01:55,570 --> 00:01:58,848
models may be the best. The model confidence set runs a

32
00:01:58,948 --> 00:02:02,901
knockout tournament. In each round we test whether the models left

33
00:02:03,001 --> 00:02:06,462
are equally good, and if not, the worst one is eliminated.

34
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:08,712
Here D is significantly worse than the

35
00:02:08,812 --> 00:02:10,691
average of the field, and leaves.

36
00:02:11,266 --> 00:02:12,603
Then C is eliminated.

37
00:02:14,666 --> 00:02:16,475
With A and B left, the test no

38
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:19,529
longer rejects: the data cannot tell them apart.

39
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:23,738
The survivors form a ninety percent confidence set for

40
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:26,714
the best model. A short list means the data

41
00:02:26,814 --> 00:02:29,691
discriminate; a long one means they do not.

42
00:02:30,766 --> 00:02:33,509
So: the best of many always looks brilliant. Judge

43
00:02:33,609 --> 00:02:36,011
it, not against the bar for one model chosen

44
00:02:36,111 --> 00:02:39,025
in advance, but against the distribution of the best.

